## Obligations: a bound is not a duty

`[transform&#124;contract:preserves(...)]` bounds which item kinds a write-back may touch — the blast radius. `[transform&#124;contract:obliges(...)]` closes the other direction: a permitted touch still has to be *justified*. The vocabulary is deliberately closed and re-decidable — `kind-stable` (the collateral rm/add kind multisets must agree) and `matched-only` (every removed non-site item must contain an invocation the transform fires on). Declarations are validated at declaration time (KORU180 refuses unknown predicates and `contract:obliges` on non-transforms); enforcement is unconditional (KORU182 refuses a violated obligation whether or not witnesses are emitted). Under enforce, each obligation commits a `transform-oblige` line the Lean checker re-decides — it does not trust the `ok` verdict.

One honest consequence: obligations are enforced at canonical-digest granularity, so a transform that *clones* items it does not change registers them as touched — clone drift reads as a touch. The satisfied pin passes untouched items through by value, which is the pattern the obligation exists to force: clone only what you change.

